        "thinking": "I see! `var extname = __require(\"path\").extname;` — it's calling `__require(\"path\")`. This is the global `__require` helper that tries `require` at runtime. Since `path` is in our external list, esbuild should map this... but it's not.\n\nWait, I just realized: `path` (without `node:` prefix) IS in our `CF_PROVIDED_BUILTINS` list. But let me check if esbuild generates an import for it...\n\nLooking at the imports in the bundle:\n```\nimport { sep } from \"path\";\nimport { join } from \"path\";\n\n\n```\n\nThese are for ESM modules that use `import { sep } from \"path\"`. But the `__require` function for CJS modules doesn't map to these imports.\n\nI think I see the problem now. When esbuild processes CJS modules, it wraps them in a `__commonJS` function that uses a runtime `__require` for external dependencies, while ESM modules get proper `import` statements. The `__require` function checks if `require` exists at runtime, but in CF Workers that compatibility layer might not be working as expected.\n\nLooking at the Cloudflare docs, `require` isn't actually a global in CF Workers ESM mode — you have to import `createRequire` from the module first. So when `__require` checks `typeof require !== \"undefined\"`, it returns false and falls through to throwing an error. The real issue is that CF Workers doesn't provide a global `require` function, so I need to either inject one in the banner or find another way to handle the CJS module's require calls.\n\n```\n\njavascript\n// Injecting a require function in the banner that maps Node.js built-ins to CF Workers equivalents\n```\n\nLet me check what dependencies mime-types, type-is, and multer actually need — mime-types uses `path` and `mime`, so I need to understand the full dependency chain to figure out the best shim strategy.\n\nActually, I realize the core issue: these are CommonJS modules that call `require()` at runtime, but there's no global `require` in CF Workers. The cleanest approach is to inject a global `require` function in the esbuild banner that maps known Node.js modules to their CF Workers equivalents, and throws an error for anything unsupported. The problem is that `import` statements are static, so I can't dynamically resolve them the same way. I need to step back and think about this more practically. The real issue is that `mime-types` is probably a CommonJS-only package, so I need a way to provide a working `require` function. I could use an esbuild plugin to inject the necessary node modules at the top of the bundle and then define a `require` polyfill that maps module IDs to those imported modules.\n\nBuilding the require polyfill... imports, those imports would be in the bundle.\n\n**Option B: Change external list to not include path/crypto/etc. from the CJS side**\n\nIf we DON'T externalize `path` and `crypto`, esbuild would bundle them. But CF Workers already provides them — bundling them would be redundant but would at least work.\n\nActually, option B doesn't work because CF Workers' nodejs_compat needs the external modules to be CF's built-in ones.\n\n**Option C: Use `inject` with a require polyfill** — this is the best approach.\n\nLet me create the inject file and add it to the esbuild config.\n\nActually wait, there's a simpler option I haven't considered: **banner with a module map**.\n\nThe banner content is raw JavaScript placed at the top. If I place some initial setup code... but I can't use `import` in the banner.\n\nOK so `inject` is the way to go. I can create a polyfill file that imports the Node modules and sets up a global `require` function that maps module IDs to those imports. The key thing is that esbuild processes injected files as part of the bundle, so they can contain imports. But I need to understand exactly how esbuild's `inject` feature works. for replacing global variable references, not for arbitrary code. Let me re-read...\n\nActually, esbuild's `inject` replaces all references to a symbol with the